content.text:
Interesting! tackx3 gets to round 24 with 62 lives and then dies, despite 20k money because the build order stopped at 6 towers. It saved money instead of expanding. The economy is now very generous (20k unspent!). The problem is spending strategy and/or late waves. A human would spend that money. The "mix" dies at 14 despite 3k unspent because it waits for next plan item (ninja? no, plan tack max then ninja max; after tack max it seems stuck? It has tack4, ninja1, etc. because it bought next type only when affordable but didn't upgrade? Actually it has 3k unspent at round 14, because plan idx? It seems it bought all 5 towers but didn't upgrade them? Wait plan mix = ['tack','ninja','bomb','sniper','tack']. After tack max, idx=1, buys ninja; current=last ninja not maxed, upgrades only if money >= cost. Should upgrade. But log shows n1 at round 14 with 3k cash. Why? Maybe it has multiple towers of type 'ninja'? No. Hmm, after buying a tower, `idx++`; next iteration current = last of type. If money >= upgrade, upgrades. It should. But it's not. Wait the order of checks: if idx<plan.length, type=plan[idx]. For idx=2 (bomb), built of type bomb and current bomb; if not maxed, upgrade. Yet log shows bomb1, sniper1, etc. with 3k. That means after buying a type, idx++ in the same inner loop, then next type, and money is enough, buys next type, until plan exhausted, and only then no more upgrades because idx==plan.length. Right — it buys all towers first because each purchase sets did and idx++ then loops, but never revisits previous types. It only upgrades current when it can't buy the next type. So it buys all towers before upgrading. Bad logic. Need to upgrade current to max before moving on (i.e., don't idx++ on buy; current stays that type and upgrades next iteration). But then for the next tower of the same type, we'd upgrade existing instead of buying a new one. Use a per-plan action state.

Simpler: process plan sequentially:
- start with current plan index, and find the last tower of that type that is not maxed; if exists, upgrade; else buy new and don't advance idx until maxed.
But if plan has multiple same type, after maxing first, current is still same type and maxed; then buy new, max, then advance. To distinguish, use a `count` per plan item.

Let's rewrite sequential:
```
let planIdx = 0;
const builtCount = {};
while (planIdx < plan.length) {
  const type = plan[planIdx];
  const n = builtCount[type] || 0;
  const towers = g.towers.filter(t=>t.type===type && t.level<3);
  // Actually we want towers that are part of this build and not maxed
  if (towers.length) { upgrade the last one }
  else { if (g.towers.filter(t=>t.type===type).length <= n) buy new; else planIdx++ }
}
```
Simpler: plan is a flat list of "build tower and max it" instructions, duplicated types. Track actions: each plan entry is a tower instance. At any time, if current tower (target) exists and not maxed, upgrade; else if it's maxed, move to next plan entry. Current target = tower at index among build-created towers, but free scout is dart etc.

Let's track `built` array of towers we bought, plus `planIdx`. For each plan entry we either need to create a tower (if not yet created for this entry) or max it. Use `targetTower` variable.
```
let target = null;
while true:
  if (!target) {
    if (planIdx >= plan.length) break;
    if (g.money >= cost) { const nt = buy(plan[planIdx]); if (nt) target = nt; planIdx++? }
  }
```
But `buy` returns bool, and tryPlace pushes to g.towers. Get last tower. Then max target; when maxed, target=null and planIdx++? If planIdx increments on purchase, current target still tracked. After maxed, target=null, planIdx already points to next. That works:
```
if (!target) {
  if (planIdx >= plan.length) break;
  const type = plan[planIdx];
  if (g.money >= cost) { const n = g.towers.length; if (buy(type)) { target = g.towers[g.towers.length-1]; planIdx++; } else planIdx++; }
  break;
} else {
  if (!target.maxed) { if (money >= cost) upgrade; break; }
  else target = null; // continue loop to buy next
}
```
Good.

Let's test this. Also a late-game strategy: after plan exhausted, upgrade all towers / buy more maxed towers if money. Could append repeats of plan or allow extra towers. For simplicity, plan can be long: tack x8 maybe. But 8 maxed Ring of Fire (21k) plus money. Or include support. Let's test several long plans:
- tack x6, bomb, sniper, ninja
- tack x4, bomb x2, sniper, ninja
- ninja x4, tack x4, bomb x2
- mix tack/ninja/bomb/sniper x2 each.

Given income ~50k potential, maxing 6 tacks = 16k, fine. Could win.

But more importantly, we need the *natural* AI or reasonable player to win. A player might naturally buy many towers and level them. Our earlier "balanced" AI spread itself too thin. The game is designed so focusing is key; that's okay. But the early difficulty wall at 13 means a player must focus a strong tower quickly. Ring of Fire rush is the clearest path and can reach 24 even with a broken build order. A human can do better and win. That might be acceptable.

Still, I'd like to reduce the wall feel. We have already increased starting cash and rewards, which helped. But now unspent cash is huge at late game (20k), suggesting rewards too generous relative to spending opportunities (limited by build order, not by slots). A human can spend it. Maybe keep.

Let's test a proper sequential max build with long plans to see if campaign is winnable.

